{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 32, :comments ["<b>Let there be no wrath, etc. — only this once.</b> Here too he magnified [the matter] by pleading merit on behalf of twenty; therefore he said ״אַל יִחַר,״ for he prays that [HaShem] plead his merit. Furthermore, he equated the hand of the servant to the hand of his master, and therefore you find that he said ״וַאֲדַבְּרָה״ and did not say ״אֶל אֲדֹנָי״ — to say that this prayer is on behalf of my own merit. And the word <b>אַךְ</b> ('only') limits, [meaning] that he would not return to pray again — perhaps one would be missing from the ten, just as he asked, perhaps five would be missing from the fifty. Or it may be because one does not withhold [punishment], as we find that HaShem said (Deuteronomy 25:3) ״אַרְבָּעִים יַכֶּנּוּ״ and the Sages came and reduced one (Makkot 22b)."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 18, :notes {0 "In the phrase וּבָצְרֵי חֲדָא (Aramaic: 'they reduced one'), the reference is to the Rabbinic decree reducing the maximum lashes from forty to thirty-nine; rendered hyper-literally following the Aramaic."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:42:17.662998877Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}